msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ (¬ P ∨ Q) ↔ (P → Q) $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L01_equiv
msgid "This theorem is the so-called **Conditional Law**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Or_imp`
#. §1: ```
#. rewrite[← Or_imp,← Or_imp]
#. ```
#. §2: `rw`
#. §3: `rw`
#. §4: `rewrite`
#. §5: `rfl`
#. §6: `rw`
#. §7: `rewrite`
#. §8: ```
#. rw[← Or_imp,← Or_imp]
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "That was a good first step. You might want to apply the **Conditional Law** codified in §0 two more times. You can do this in one step with:\n"
"§1\n"
"Or even better ...\n"
"### **❯ The §2 tactic**\n"
"The §3 tactic is the same as §4 but it tries to apply §5 at the end to clear the goal. For this reason, we **almost always** use §6 instead of §7. 👉 Try:\n"
"§8\n"
""
msgstr ""

#. §0: `x = x`
#. §1: `P ↔ P`
#. §2: `x ~ x`
#. §3: `∼`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L01_equiv
msgid "This tactic clears a goal whose target has the form §0 or §1 (or §2 where §3 is any relation that has a reflexivity lemma tagged with the attribute @[refl] -- we'll talk more about relations later)."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rewrite[e]`
#. §1: `e`
#. §2: `e`
#. §3: `←`
#. §4: `<-`
#. §5: `e : a = b`
#. §6: `rewrite[e]`
#. §7: `a`
#. §8: `b`
#. §9: `rewrite[← e]`
#. §10: `b`
#. §11: `a`
#. §12: `e₁`
#. §13: `e₂`
#. §14: `eₙ`
#. §15: ```
#. rewrite [e₁, ..., eₙ]
#. ```
#. §16: `rewrite [e] at l`
#. §17: `e`
#. §18: `l`
#. §19: `l`
#. §20: `⊢`
#. §21: `|-`
#. §22: `←`
#. §23: `\\left`
#. §24: `⊢`
#. §25: `\\vdash`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L01_equiv
msgid "The tactic §0 applies the identity/equivalence §1 as a **rewrite** rule to the target.\n"
"\n"
"If §2 is preceded by left arrow (§3 or §4), the rewrite is applied in the reverse direction. For example, if §5 then ...\n"
"* §6 replaces all instances of §7 with §8 in the target\n"
"* §9 replaces all instances of §10 with §11 in the target\n"
"\n"
"If §12,§13,...,§14 is a sequence of identities then\n"
"§15\n"
"applies the given rules sequentially.\n"
"\n"
"Finally, §16 rewrites §17 at location(s) §18, where §19 is a list of hypotheses in the local context or the main goal §20 (or §21).\n"
"\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting tip**\n"
"To pretty print the left-arrow §22 use §23. To pretty print the symbol §24 use §25."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "🔍 Check out two freshly unlocked laws of logic in the **Theorems** tab."
msgstr ""

#. §0: ```
#. (H₁ ∧ H₂) → P
#. ```
#. §1: ```
#. H₁ → (H₂ → P)
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "### **🤔 Level 2?**\n"
"Oftentimes, we'll have implications of the form\n"
"§0\n"
"For programmatic purposes, this kind of proposition is better expressed in the following way:\n"
"§1\n"
"This exercise demonstrates that this process of translating the first form into the second (called **currying**) is logically valid."
msgstr ""

